WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.667 --> 00:00:02.667
-Our next guest is
the award-winning host

00:00:02.667 --> 00:00:04.000
of "The Graham Norton Show."

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:06.033
The second season of his show,
"Queen of the Universe,"

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:08.734
will premiere on June 2nd
on Paramount Plus.

00:00:08.734 --> 00:00:11.200
Please welcome back to the show,
our friend from across the pond,

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.601
Graham Norton, everybody!

00:00:13.601 --> 00:00:17.868
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:17.868 --> 00:00:25.834
♪♪

00:00:25.834 --> 00:00:27.400
-Welcome.
-Thank you very much.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.033
-Welcome, welcome, welcome.
This is a big deal for us.

00:00:31.033 --> 00:00:33.067
-Wow. That's -- Yeah, that's --

00:00:33.067 --> 00:00:34.300
[ Cheering ]

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:38.033
It's impressive
that they can cheer

00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:40.033
and whoop like that
while Googling me.

00:00:40.033 --> 00:00:41.534
-Oh, no, no, no.

00:00:41.534 --> 00:00:43.601
See, you don't realize
you're a big deal over here

00:00:43.601 --> 00:00:45.367
and people are very excited.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:00:45.367 --> 00:00:46.868
[ Cheering ]

00:00:46.868 --> 00:00:49.434
-I think a lot of them think
they would have to

00:00:49.434 --> 00:00:51.501
cross the Atlantic to see you.
-Take a plane.

00:00:51.501 --> 00:00:53.067
-And now they get you here
in person.

00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:56.200
-Here I am.
-Here you are.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:58.801
-And I'm very happy to be here.
I love New York City.

00:00:58.801 --> 00:01:01.601
I think it's very good
for old people fun.

00:01:01.601 --> 00:01:03.667
-Oh, wow. Interesting.
Meaning what?

00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:05.834
-Well, you know,
my days of daytime clubbing

00:01:05.834 --> 00:01:07.634
and zip-lining, they're over.

00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:08.868
-And you were
very good at them both.

00:01:08.868 --> 00:01:11.334
-I loved them.
-Were you a zip liner for real?

00:01:11.334 --> 00:01:12.901
-No.
-Okay.

00:01:12.901 --> 00:01:14.634
-You went to some clubs
in the daytime, though.

00:01:14.634 --> 00:01:15.734
-That I did, too.
-Yeah.

00:01:15.734 --> 00:01:17.767
-So now I'm here,

00:01:17.767 --> 00:01:19.400
and, you know,
you can go to see shows.

00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.167
There's galleries.
They deliver things.

00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:23.968
It's easy to get a cab.
It's old people fun.

00:01:23.968 --> 00:01:26.000
And if you are
an older thrill-seeker,

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.501
you can be excited
by maybe being hit by a bike,

00:01:28.501 --> 00:01:31.934
because that gets
your heart rate up.

00:01:31.934 --> 00:01:34.100
-That's the new zip-line.
-Yeah, that is it.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.634
-Yeah.
Ooh! Where's my medication?

00:01:35.634 --> 00:01:38.467
-You obviously, you usually
are asking the questions.

00:01:38.467 --> 00:01:39.801
How do you feel about

00:01:39.801 --> 00:01:42.067
being the interviewee
as opposed to the interviewer?

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:45.400
-I mean, not great,
to be honest --

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:46.901
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.901 --> 00:01:50.868
So sorry, apologies in advance.
That bit's so much easier.

00:01:50.868 --> 00:01:53.100
It's so much easier to be you.
-I agree.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.300
I just did James Corden's
show last week,

00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.234
and I genuinely said to him,

00:01:57.234 --> 00:02:00.434
"Being the guest
is so much harder."

00:02:00.434 --> 00:02:02.501
-Really, really hard.
So bear that in mind.

00:02:02.501 --> 00:02:04.000
[ Cheers ]

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:06.200
This is very difficult.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:07.601
[ Applause ]

00:02:07.601 --> 00:02:09.334
-And I think that, you know,

00:02:09.334 --> 00:02:10.968
sometimes I'll have people,

00:02:10.968 --> 00:02:12.667
often will be on the show
and when it's over,

00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:14.100
they'll lean over and go, "How
do you do this every night?"

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:16.167
And I'm like, doing it
every night is the easy part.

00:02:16.167 --> 00:02:18.701
Doing it once every six months,
that's the hard part.

00:02:18.701 --> 00:02:20.601
-Yeah, no, I remember
when I was growing up,

00:02:20.601 --> 00:02:23.234
there was a show in Ireland
called "The Late Late Show,"

00:02:23.234 --> 00:02:27.033
and I mean,
it started about 7:00, but...

00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:30.234
but it was dark out, so...

00:02:30.234 --> 00:02:32.501
And there was
a host called Gay Byrne.

00:02:32.501 --> 00:02:33.968
That was actually his name.

00:02:33.968 --> 00:02:36.734
And he did it forever.
The show is still going.

00:02:36.734 --> 00:02:38.601
And as a kid, I always dreamt of

00:02:38.601 --> 00:02:41.033
being a guest on the show
and it's only as an adult,

00:02:41.033 --> 00:02:43.200
I've realized it's much better
to be the host,

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:45.300
because you get to be
on every week.

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:47.801
And also, you know,

00:02:47.801 --> 00:02:49.601
the chances of you
coming back are higher,

00:02:49.601 --> 00:02:50.834
because --
you know what it's like.

00:02:50.834 --> 00:02:52.167
When you started this show,

00:02:52.167 --> 00:02:54.100
there were probably guests
you really wanted to get.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.934
-Now, not so much.
-Yup, exactly.

00:02:56.934 --> 00:02:58.133
The longer you do it,
you want it --

00:02:58.133 --> 00:02:59.934
you start repeating people
and other people

00:02:59.934 --> 00:03:01.334
sort of get
dropped off the ledger.

00:03:01.334 --> 00:03:02.534
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:02.534 --> 00:03:04.501
-But the thing
that I'm very impressed with,

00:03:04.501 --> 00:03:07.200
because I do think you have a
harder job as a host than I do,

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:10.534
is you have to navigate
multiple guests out at once.

00:03:10.534 --> 00:03:12.567
And it's sometimes
as many as six people.

00:03:12.567 --> 00:03:14.801
Also six very famous people.

00:03:14.801 --> 00:03:17.000
Do you get nervous
about that part of it?

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:18.367
-Well, sometimes.

00:03:18.367 --> 00:03:22.033
But I think what's good
about actors is -- no offense,

00:03:22.033 --> 00:03:23.934
but sometimes they don't
care about an audience.

00:03:23.934 --> 00:03:25.167
-Right.

00:03:25.167 --> 00:03:27.367
-But they do care
about each other.

00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:28.434
-I see.

00:03:28.434 --> 00:03:30.934
-So if an actor
tells a funny story,

00:03:30.934 --> 00:03:34.701
you can see someone seething,
kind of like,

00:03:34.701 --> 00:03:39.200
"I have a funny story!
I [bleep] myself!"

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:42.534
And so the stakes
get higher and higher.

00:03:42.534 --> 00:03:45.734
Sometimes we do get, like,
terrible stories,

00:03:45.734 --> 00:03:47.567
like, don't tell that
on television!

00:03:47.567 --> 00:03:50.267
-Well, that is fascinating,
because I will say,

00:03:50.267 --> 00:03:52.834
in my head,
when I think of famous people

00:03:52.834 --> 00:03:54.801
and the most embarrassing story
they've told on a chat show,

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:56.667
it's always on yours.

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:59.534
-But I think that is
because of the other guests.

00:03:59.534 --> 00:04:00.968
They're trying to outdo
each other.

00:04:00.968 --> 00:04:03.300
And also it is funny to say
something really rude

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.300
in front of Dame Judi Dench
or something.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:08.234
-And I do believe she is
someone a perfect example.

00:04:08.234 --> 00:04:10.534
She knows the role she plays.
-Yes.

00:04:10.534 --> 00:04:12.567
-When someone says something,
she knows to

00:04:12.567 --> 00:04:14.901
lean into the Dame
in those moments.

00:04:14.901 --> 00:04:17.000
-Absolutely. "Ooh!"

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:20.000
-Do you ever -- I always,
if I ever get nervous --

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.200
I think the longer
I do the show,

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:22.701
the less I get nervous,
which is wonderful.

00:04:22.701 --> 00:04:25.567
But it is the sort
of very famous people,

00:04:25.567 --> 00:04:27.767
especially famous people
when I was young,

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:29.701
and you tend
to get a lot of those.

00:04:29.701 --> 00:04:32.067
Do you get nervous anymore
with people like that?

00:04:32.067 --> 00:04:34.100
-I mean, sometimes.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:35.200
Backstage, we were talking
about Tom Cruise.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:36.501
-Yeah.

00:04:36.501 --> 00:04:38.033
-And Tom Cruise is one
of those people, you know --

00:04:38.033 --> 00:04:40.267
Because normally once
they come through that door

00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:42.834
and they sit down,
pretty quickly,

00:04:42.834 --> 00:04:45.634
it's just a man in a jacket
or some woman in a frock,

00:04:45.634 --> 00:04:47.167
and that's the end of it.

00:04:47.167 --> 00:04:51.167
Tom Cruise, I never get past
the fact that it's Tom Cruise.

00:04:51.167 --> 00:04:54.634
He remains Tom Cruise
throughout the interview,

00:04:54.634 --> 00:04:57.968
and an audience likes that,
and I enjoy that,

00:04:57.968 --> 00:05:00.667
and also, you know,
the other guests, you can tell

00:05:00.667 --> 00:05:02.667
they're all a bit starry
about him,

00:05:02.667 --> 00:05:04.634
and there aren't many people
like that.

00:05:04.634 --> 00:05:06.701
That kind of old star system's
kind of gone.

00:05:06.701 --> 00:05:08.801
-It's really funny
how you would say,

00:05:08.801 --> 00:05:10.300
"Oh, there's no
movie stars anymore.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:12.067
There's just great actors
and actresses."

00:05:12.067 --> 00:05:13.501
And then every now
and then you're like,

00:05:13.501 --> 00:05:14.834
"Oh, I just saw
a [bleep] movie star."

00:05:14.834 --> 00:05:17.434
Like, they come out,
the audience is different.

00:05:17.434 --> 00:05:18.667
It's really, it's a trip.

00:05:18.667 --> 00:05:22.334
-Like, Jennifer Lopez
is not everywoman.

00:05:22.334 --> 00:05:23.968
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]

00:05:23.968 --> 00:05:26.133
-It's so funny
that she played a maid.

00:05:26.133 --> 00:05:28.767
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.767 --> 00:05:31.467
Yeah, right.

00:05:31.467 --> 00:05:33.067
-When you come on

00:05:33.067 --> 00:05:34.200
another one
of these shows like this,

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:36.067
do you sort of check out
the green rooms?

00:05:36.067 --> 00:05:38.100
Are you measuring them up
compared to yours?

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.634
-I have to say, already now,
I have to go back to London

00:05:41.634 --> 00:05:43.267
and I may have
to change my ways.

00:05:43.267 --> 00:05:45.901
A handwritten note
from Seth Meyers.

00:05:45.901 --> 00:05:48.167
-I should note I learned
the handwritten notes,

00:05:48.167 --> 00:05:49.834
that was a gift given to me.

00:05:49.834 --> 00:05:51.601
Jimmy Fallon told me, "Make
sure, when you get this job,

00:05:51.601 --> 00:05:52.767
you leave a --"

00:05:52.767 --> 00:05:53.834
So I leave a handwritten note
every day. Yeah.

00:05:53.834 --> 00:05:54.934
[ Crowd awws ]

00:05:54.934 --> 00:05:57.300
-See, what's sweet is,
I used to do it.

00:05:57.300 --> 00:05:58.634
[ Laughter ]

00:05:58.634 --> 00:06:01.067
-So this isn't a thing
you didn't know about.

00:06:01.067 --> 00:06:03.367
You just stopped.
-I just stopped.

00:06:03.367 --> 00:06:04.834
It's like, very nice of you

00:06:04.834 --> 00:06:06.400
to swing by the dressing room,
say hello.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:07.434
Don't do that anymore, either.

00:06:07.434 --> 00:06:09.000
-You don't even say
hi to the guests?

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:10.033
-What's the other thing
I don't do?

00:06:10.033 --> 00:06:12.467
Oh, talk to them afterwards.

00:06:12.467 --> 00:06:15.100
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:17.000
[ Cheering ]
No, no.

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:19.334
If I bump into them
in a corridor, I'll say hello.

00:06:19.334 --> 00:06:21.000
-Oh that's lovely.
-Yeah.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:22.467
-I mean,
that's above and beyond.

00:06:22.467 --> 00:06:24.767
-Sometimes, though,
I don't recognize them,

00:06:24.767 --> 00:06:26.100
and I'll think,
"They're well dressed."

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:28.601
[ Laughter ]

00:06:28.601 --> 00:06:33.567
-I once saw -- A young person
on the street said hi to me,

00:06:33.567 --> 00:06:36.868
and I panicked and I thought
they had been a young actor

00:06:36.868 --> 00:06:38.968
who'd been on the show
and I'd forgotten.

00:06:38.968 --> 00:06:40.400
And we were waiting
for the light.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:43.701
So I leaned in and I go,
"So what have you been up to?"

00:06:43.701 --> 00:06:46.868
And they looked at me and go,
"Just getting ready for finals."

00:06:46.868 --> 00:06:49.601
[ Laughter ]

00:06:49.601 --> 00:06:51.000
I was like, "Cool, cool.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.734
That's a normal question
that a man my age asks."

00:06:53.734 --> 00:06:57.234
-Yeah. "You busy right now?"

00:06:57.234 --> 00:06:58.567
-Hey, I got a lot
more questions.

00:06:58.567 --> 00:07:00.968
You guys, that's Graham Norton!
We'll be right back.

